 8. e3 {Supports a later d4 and keeps the structure flexible.}
 8... c5 {Space in the centre.}
 9. Nc3 {Develops and adds pressure on d5.}
 9... Nc6 {Develops, controlling d4 and e5.}
 10. cxd5 {Releases the tension at a good moment, aiming to leave Black with an isolated or hanging d-pawn.}
 10... exd5 {[%csl Rd5,Rc5] Black recaptures toward the centre. The c5/d5 "hanging pawns" are active but can become targets.})
7. Bb2 {[%cal Gb2f6] The bishop takes aim at the kingside along the long diagonal.}
7... Nc6 {Develops and controls d4 and e5.}
8. e3 {Holds d4 and keeps the option of cxd5 or d4 at the right moment.}
8... b6 {Prepares ...Bb7 to contest the long diagonal.}
9. Nc3 {Develops and adds pressure on d5.}
9... Bb7 {Every piece is out. Both bishops face each other on the long diagonal.}
10. cxd5 {Releases the tension so Black's d5 has to be recaptured by a pawn.}
10... exd5 {[%csl Rc5,Rd5] Black gets "hanging pawns" on c5 and d5: strong in the centre, but they need constant protection.}
11. d4 {[%cal Gd4c5,Gb2d4] Attacks c5 at once. If Black plays ...c4, White gets the b3 lever and e5. Otherwise dxc5 leaves an isolated d-pawn.} *

[Event "vs 1...d5: 2.g3 (King's Indian Attack)"]

{White builds a King's Indian setup with g3, Bg2, O-O, d3, Nbd2 and e4. The plan is a kingside attack with e5, h4 and Nf1-h2-g4, or a central e4 break.}
1. Nf3 {[%csl Ge5,Gd4] A flexible start that keeps every system available.}
1... d5 {Black takes the centre.}
2. g3 {[%cal Gf1g2] The King's Indian Attack. White fianchettoes first and aims for e4 later.}
2... Nf6 {Natural development.}
(2... c6 {Prepares ...Bg4 with the d5-pawn solidly supported.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto.}
 3... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] The bishop develops actively and pins against e2 and d1.}
 4. O-O {King safety, then d3/Nbd2 and e4.}
 4... Nd7 {Supports ...e5 to build a big centre.}
 5. d3 {[%csl Ge4] Prepares e4.}
 5... Ngf6 {Develops.}
 6. Nbd2 {[%cal Gd2e4] Supports e4.}
 6... e5 {Black has the ideal centre with pawns on c6, d5 and e5.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] White's thematic break, challenging d5.}
 7... Be7 {Solid development.}
 8. h3 {Asks the bishop to decide: exchange on f3 or retreat.}
 8... Bh5 {Keeps the pin, but the bishop could get stranded after g4.}
 9. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e5] The rook backs up e4 and puts indirect pressure on e5.})
(2... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Black develops the bishop early, before ...e6.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto.}
 3... Nd7 {Prepares ...e5 and ...Ngf6.}
 4. O-O {King safety.}
 4... c6 {Supports d5.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4.}
 5... e5 {Black's centre is impressive, but White aims to undermine it.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4.}
 6... Ngf6 {Development.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] Challenges d5 and opens lines for the g2-bishop.}
 7... Be7 {Development.}
 8. h3 {Asks the g4-bishop to commit.}
 8... Bh5 {Keeps the pin.})
(2... c5 {A reversed King's Indian: Black plays in the centre aggressively.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto.}
 3... Nc6 {Develops and supports ...e5.}
 4. O-O {King safety.}
 4... e5 {[%csl Gd4] Black has a big centre. White aims to undermine it with c3, e4 and Re1.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4 and keeps the d3-pawn compact.}
 5... Nf6 {Develops.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4.}
 6... Be7 {Solid.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] The break that challenges Black's centre.}
 7... O-O {Black castles.}
 8. Re1 {Pressure on e5 and support for e4.}
 8... d4 {[%csl Ge4,Rc3] Black closes the centre. White will play a4/Nc4 or prepare f4.})
(2... g6 {A symmetrical setup.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto.}
 3... Bg7 {Black contests the long diagonal.}
 4. O-O {King safety.}
 4... e5 {Black takes space.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4.}
 5... Ne7 {Keeps the f-pawn free for ...f5.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4.}
 6... O-O {Castles.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] Challenges the centre.}
 7... c5 {Black grabs more space.})
3. Bg2 {Fianchetto, the backbone of the KIA.}
3... e6 {A French-style setup, the most common way to meet the KIA.}
(3... c6 {Solid. Black prepares ...Bg4.}
 4. O-O {King safety.}
 4... Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Active development.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4.}
 5... Nbd7 {Supports ...e5.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4.}
 6... e5 {Black's ideal centre.}
 7. e4 {[%cal Ge4d5] Challenges it immediately.}
 7... dxe4 {Black releases the tension and opens the d-file.}
 8. dxe4 {[%csl Gd5] Recaptures. White's knight can later use c4 or f1-e3 to reach d5 or f5.}
 8... Bc5 {[%cal Gc5f2] An active bishop aiming at f2.}
 9. h3 {Questions the g4-bishop before it pins anything.})
(3... Bf5 {The bishop comes out before ...e6.}
 4. O-O {King safety.}
 4... e6 {Black has a London-style setup.}
 5. d3 {[%csl Ge4] Prepares Nbd2 and e4, hitting the f5-bishop.}
 5... h6 {Makes a retreat square on h7.}
 6. Nbd2 {Supports e4.}
 6... Be7 {Development.}
 7. Qe1 {[%cal Ge1e4] A typical KIA idea: the queen backs up e4 from e1.}
 7... O-O {Black castles.}
