[Event "vs 1...f5 (Dutch)"]

{Against the Dutch, White plays d3 and e4 to blow open the e-file and the weakened e8–h5 diagonal.}
1. Nf3 {[%csl Ge5,Gd4] A flexible start.}
1... f5 {[%csl Re6,Rg6] The Dutch. It controls e4 but weakens the e8–h5 diagonal and the king's cover.}
2. d3 {[%cal Ge2e4] Prepares e4 to open lines against Black's king.}
(2. g3 {A quieter Leningrad-style approach.}
 2... Nf6 {Development.}
 3. Bg2 {Fianchetto.}
 3... g6 {A Leningrad setup.}
 4. O-O {King safety.}
 4... Bg7 {Contests the long diagonal.}
 5. d3 {Prepares e4.}
 5... O-O {Castles.}
 6. c4 {Gains queenside space.}
 6... d6 {Development.}
 7. Nc3 {Development.})
(2. d4 {Transposes to a Dutch with 1.d4.}
 2... Nf6 {Development.}
 3. g3 {Fianchetto.}
 3... e6 {A Stonewall or Classical setup.}
 4. Bg2 {Fianchetto.}
 4... d5 {The Stonewall: pawns on f5, e6 and d5.}
 5. O-O {King safety.}
 5... Bd6 {[%cal Gd6h2] Eyes h2.}
 6. c4 {Challenges d5.}
 6... c6 {Completes the Stonewall.}
 7. b3 {[%cal Gc1a3] Prepares Ba3 to trade Black's good dark-squared bishop.})
2... Nf6 {Development.}
(2... d6 {Prepares ...e5.}
 3. e4 {Strikes at once.}
 3... fxe4 {Black captures.}
 4. dxe4 {Opens the d-file and the e-file.}
 4... e5 {Black builds a centre.}
 5. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4g8] Aims at the weak light squares around Black's king.}
 5... Nf6 {Development.}
 6. Ng5 {[%cal Gg5f7] Threatens Nf7, forking the queen and the h8-rook.}
 6... d5 {Blocks the bishop's diagonal.}
 7. exd5 {Captures.})
(2... Nc6 {Development.}
 3. e4 {Strikes at once.}
 3... fxe4 {Black captures.}
 4. dxe4 {Recaptures.}
 4... e5 {Black builds a centre.}
 5. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4g8] Aims at the weak light squares.}
 5... Nf6 {Development.}
 6. O-O {King safety.}
 6... d6 {Development.}
 7. Ng5 {[%cal Gg5f7] Threatens Nf7.})
3. e4 {[%cal Ge4f5] The thematic break.}
3... fxe4 {Black captures.}
4. dxe4 {Recaptures. The e-file and the e8–h5 diagonal are opening up.}
4... e5 {Black builds a centre.}
(4... Nxe4 {Grabs a pawn, but White gains a big lead in development.}
 5. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3e4] Develops with tempo on the e4-knight.}
 5... Nf6 {Retreats.}
 6. Ng5 {[%cal Gd3h7,Gg5h7] White aims at h7 with a menacing attack.}
 6... g6 {Defends h7.}
 7. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Brings the h-pawn into the attack.})
(4... d6 {Development.}
 5. e5 {[%csl Ge5] Gains space.}
 5... dxe5 {Black captures.}
 6. Qxd8+ {Trades queens with check.}
 6... Kxd8 {Black loses the right to castle.}
 7. Nxe5 {White wins back the pawn, and Black's king is stuck in the centre.}
 7... Ke8 {The king steps to safety.}
 8. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4f7] Hits f7.}
 8... e6 {Blocks.}
 9. Nf7 {[%cal Gf7h8] Forks the rook.}
 9... Rg8 {The rook escapes.})
5. Bc4 {[%cal Gc4g8] Pressure on the light squares.}
5... c6 {Prepares ...d5.}
6. Nc3 {Development.}
6... Be7 {Development.}
7. O-O {King safety.}
7... d6 {Development.}
8. Ng5 {[%cal Gg5f7] Threatens Nf7, forking the queen and the h8-rook. Black must react precisely.} *

[Event "vs 1...d5: 2.g3 ...Bf5 setups"]

{When Black develops the bishop to f5 early, White targets b7 with c4, cxd5 and Qb3.}
1. Nf3 {[%csl Ge5,Gd4] A flexible start.}
1... d5 {Black takes the centre.}
2. g3 {Fianchetto.}
2... Bf5 {The bishop develops early.}
3. Bg2 {Fianchetto.}
3... e6 {A London-style setup.}
4. O-O {King safety.}
4... Nf6 {Development.}
5. d3 {Prepares e4.}
5... h6 {Makes a retreat square on h7.}
6. c4 {Challenges d5.}
6... c6 {Supports d5.}
7. cxd5 {Opens the c-file.}
7... exd5 {The bishop on f5 now leaves b7 unguarded.}
8. Nc3 {Development.}
8... Be7 {Development.}
9. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7] Attacks b7, which the f5-bishop left behind.}
9... Qb6 {Defends.}
10. Be3 {Development with tempo on the queen.} *

